      "thinking": "The real issue is that the third-party dependency sits on the render path with no timeout or circuit breaker, so a provider outage takes down every account page. The fix is to make that card optional: degrade gracefully by rendering the page without it, setting card_last_four to None and surfacing a provider_failure flag alongside any cache_failure, with a timeout/circuit breaker as a further safeguard.\n\nI realize degradation alone won't fix the latency spike since the 2s hang per request remains \u2014 but timeouts belong to the caller-supplied fetch function, which is outside this repo's control. I'll scope the fix to graceful degradation plus reporting, since that resolves the error rate, and note the timeout limitation explicitly rather than over-engineering a circuit breaker that adds statefulness and testing complexity.\n\nNow I'm updating payment_provider's docstring to describe how the page renders without card data during an outage instead of failing outright.\n\nFor account_page.py, I'll add a nullable `provider_failure` field to RenderedPage and wrap the card lookup in a helper that catches the provider exception, returning the card plus None on success or None plus a descriptive error string (naming host/status) on failure, so tests can assert the host and 503 code appear in that message.\n\nI also need to update the existing tests that assert failure is always non-None\u2014those encode the old buggy behavior\u2014so I'll rewrite them to check the page still renders successfully with a populated provider_failure instead of failing outright. I should also check other callers of card_on_file and serve_account_page to make sure nothing else breaks.\n\n",
